{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"06","chapter_title":"Course Completion and Transfer","title":"Revision Sprint","description":"CAT 100 textbook section on revision sprinting, centered on a revision sprint log, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the revision sprint log shows a defensible decision in CAT 100"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer","cat100_supplement_revision_sprint","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/08/05"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_calibration","cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_annotation_signal"],"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Revision Sprint\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Revision Sprint, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/08-06-revision-sprint.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Revision Sprint: Revision Sprint, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nYou will almost never have unlimited time to fix an artifact. You will have thirty minutes before submission, an hour before a meeting, a lunch break before a client call. In that window, the difference between a strong outcome and a broken one is not how fast you type; it is the order in which you choose to fix things. A revision sprint is the discipline of triaging a list of problems by impact, fixing correctness before cosmetics, and logging each change so the work is traceable.\n\nThe trap is that cosmetic fixes feel productive. Changing the font is satisfying and visible, so under pressure students reach for it first and leave a `#REF!` error sitting in the total cell. A reviewer who opens the file sees a broken number, not a nice font. The sprint teaches you to spend a fixed budget where it buys the most: correctness errors that make the artifact wrong, then usability, then polish, and to stop before you start something you cannot finish.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the revision sprint log shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nLee has thirty minutes before the capstone deadline and a budget spreadsheet with a broken total, a dead link on the portfolio page, plain default styling, and a suggestion from an AI coding assistant (GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, or Cursor, whichever Lee happens to use) to rebuild the whole layout with CSS grid. Everything feels urgent. The instinct is to start with the most interesting task, the grid rebuild, because it would look impressive.\n\nThat instinct would be a disaster. A CSS grid rebuild started with thirty minutes left ships an unfinished, broken layout, while the `#REF!` error and the dead link, both quick and both fatal to credibility, go unfixed. The sprint method tells Lee to triage by impact first, which puts the grid rebuild last, as future work, and the two correctness bugs first. The disciplined move, declining the rebuild and logging it as future work, is independent of which tool produced the suggestion; tool names and pricing churn (GitHub Copilot, for instance, moved to usage-based AI Credits on June 1, 2026), so the triage logic, not the tool, is the durable skill.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA revision sprint has three moves: inventory, triage, and logged execution.\n\nInventory means listing every known issue before touching anything, so you are choosing from the full set rather than reacting to whatever you see first. Triage means ranking those issues by impact, using a simple hierarchy:\n\n| Tier | Issue type | Example | Why this rank |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Correctness | `#REF!` in a total, a dead link | The artifact is wrong or broken; everything else is moot |\n| 2 | Usability and access | No heading styles, missing labels | The artifact works but is hard to use |\n| 3 | Cosmetic | Font choice, color tweaks | Nice to have, lowest payoff per minute |\n| Out | Scope-too-large | Full layout rebuild | Cannot finish safely in the budget; defer |\n\nLogged execution means recording each fix with a timestamp, location, and the change made, so a reviewer can trace exactly what happened. The changelog is also a safeguard against yourself: if a fix breaks something, you can see what you touched and when.\n\nThe principle underneath is triage under a fixed budget. You are not trying to fix everything; you are maximizing the artifact's correctness and usability within the time you have. Knowing what to leave undone, and recording it as future work rather than starting it, is as much a skill as the fixes themselves.\n\n<inline-check question=\"You have 30 minutes left. The spreadsheet shows #REF! in the total, and you also want to rebuild the page with CSS grid. What do you do first?\" explanation=\"Correctness outranks cosmetics, and a fix you cannot finish is worse than none. The #REF! is a tier-1 correctness bug; the grid rebuild is out of scope for the budget and should be logged as future work, not started with minutes left.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Start the CSS grid rebuild because it will look the most impressive.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Fix the #REF! first; log the grid rebuild as future work.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Change the font first because it is the quickest visible win.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: A 30-Minute Revision Sprint with a Changelog\n\nA student has 30 minutes to revise a budget spreadsheet and its GitHub Pages page before submission. They first list every issue, then triage by impact, then log each fix with a timestamp.\n\n**Triage list (highest impact first).**\n\n| Priority | Issue | Type |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `#REF!` in the Total cell B14 | Correctness |\n| 2 | Dead link to the data source in `index.html` | Correctness |\n| 3 | Default font, no heading styles | Cosmetic |\n| 4 | \"Rebuild layout with CSS grid\" (AI suggestion) | Out of scope |\n\n**Sprint changelog.**\n\n```\n14:05  Replaced #REF! in B14 with =SUM(B2:B13) = 8,420; checked against receipts tab. (Priority 1)\n14:13  Fixed dead <a href> in index.html line 31 to the real CSV path; link now opens. (Priority 2)\n14:22  Added <h2> styles and a readable font in styles.css. (Priority 3)\n14:28  Logged \"CSS grid rebuild\" as a FUTURE item; declined for this sprint to avoid shipping unfinished. (Priority 4)\n```\n\nThe sprint succeeds because correctness was fixed before cosmetics, and the changelog lets a reviewer trace every edit by time, location, and reason. The grid rebuild is recorded as future work rather than started with eight minutes left, which would have left the artifact broken.\n\nRun the sprint as a fixed procedure. Predict each step before revealing it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run a 30-minute revision sprint\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You open the artifact with 30 minutes left. What is the first move, before any fix?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Fix the first problem you happen to notice\",\"Inventory every known issue before touching anything\",\"Start the most interesting task to build momentum\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Inventory first: list every issue so you choose from the full set instead of reacting to whatever you see first.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Triage by impact. Label each item correctness, usability, cosmetic, or out-of-scope, and sort so tier-1 bugs rise to the top.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Execute top-down with a logged changelog: timestamp, location, the change, and a quick verification like checking the total against receipts.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Defer what you cannot finish. Record the CSS grid rebuild as future work with a one-line reason rather than half-starting it near the deadline.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the sprint moves:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Inventory every known issue before editing\",\"Triage issues into correctness, usability, cosmetic, and out-of-scope\",\"Fix correctness bugs first, logging each with a timestamp\",\"Add usability and cosmetic fixes if budget remains\",\"Record the deferred large task as future work with a reason\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThese four concepts anchor the section; trace how they connect:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\",\"cat100_supplement_revision_sprint\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nSpend your time on the highest-impact fix you can finish, and never start a fix you cannot complete in the budget. For each sprint:\n\n1. **Inventory before editing.** Write the full issue list first; do not fix the first thing you notice.\n2. **Correctness beats cosmetics.** A broken total or dead link outranks any styling change, every time.\n3. **Defer what you cannot finish.** A large rebuild started near the deadline ships broken; log it as future work instead.\n\nIf you reach the end of the budget with correctness fixed and the unfinished items recorded, the sprint succeeded even if not everything is done.\n\n**Why severity-first ordering has evidence behind it.** Research on multiple-goal pursuit shows people systematically misallocate scarce time across competing deadlines (Ballard, Vancouver, & Neal, 2018), and software-engineering triage practice orders defect work by severity for the same reason: under a fixed budget, correctness failures cost more than cosmetic ones.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is equating motion with progress. Reformatting fonts feels like work and produces a visible change, but if a `#REF!` sits in the total, you spent your scarce minutes on tier 3 while a tier 1 error remained. Impact, not visible activity, sets the order.\n\nThe second misreading is treating \"log it as future work\" as failure. Declining the CSS grid rebuild with eight minutes left is the correct, professional decision; it protects the artifact from shipping broken. Recording deferred work is part of a finished sprint, not an admission you ran out of time.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nOpen with an inventory pass: go through the artifact and write down every problem you can find, without fixing any of them yet. The broken total, the dead link, the plain styling, and the tempting big rebuild all go on the list together so you can weigh them against each other.\n\nNow triage. Label each item correctness, usability, cosmetic, or out-of-scope, and sort. The `#REF!` and the dead link rise to the top because they make the artifact wrong; the font drops to tier 3; the grid rebuild goes to out-of-scope because it cannot be finished safely in the window. Then execute top-down, logging each fix with a timestamp, the location, the change, and a quick verification, like checking the new total against the receipts tab.\n\nEnd by recording the deferred item explicitly as future work with a one-line reason. The walkthrough output is a triage table plus a timestamped changelog that lets a reviewer replay every decision, including the decision not to start the rebuild.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, take a five-item issue list of your own and triage it into the four tiers, then predict which items a thirty-minute budget would reach. A strong reader does not just follow Lee's order; they can defend why a particular fix belongs in tier 1 versus tier 3.\n\nA strong reader of the revision sprint can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the full inventory of issues, listed before any editing?\n2. Which issues are correctness, and why do they come first?\n3. What was deferred, and why was deferring it the right call?\n4. Does the changelog let a reviewer trace each fix by time, location, and verification?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe sprint is strong when correctness items are fixed first, each fix is logged with a timestamp and verification, and at least one item is consciously deferred with a reason rather than half-started. The standard is a triage table plus a changelog, not a vague \"I made some improvements.\"\n\nA useful closing note would read: \"I inventoried four issues, then fixed the two correctness bugs first: the `#REF!` in B14 (now `=SUM(B2:B13)` = 8,420, checked against receipts) and the dead data link in `index.html` line 31. I added heading styles next, then logged the CSS grid rebuild as future work because starting it with eight minutes left would have shipped a broken layout.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nRun the same sprint method on a non-coursework deadline: forty-five minutes to clean a shared report before a team meeting. The tiers shift slightly, since a wrong figure in the summary is now your tier 1 and formatting consistency is tier 3, but the logic is identical: inventory, triage by impact, fix correctness first, defer what you cannot finish. Producing a defensible triage in a context with social stakes, where a colleague will see what you chose to fix and what you left, is the test of whether the sprint is a transferable habit rather than a one-time exam trick.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the revision sprint log packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/08-06-revision-sprint-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"revision sprint log\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"08.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the revision sprint log.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the revision sprint log for CAT 100 08.06. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_revision_sprint\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the revision sprint log records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Ballard, T., Vancouver, J. B., & Neal, A. (2018). On the pursuit of multiple goals with different deadlines. Journal of Applied Psychology. https://doi.org/10.1037/apl0000304\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_revision_sprint","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_revision_sprint","cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer"],"stem":"You have a 30-minute revision sprint. Your budget sheet shows a #REF! error in the Total cell, and the page also has a plain default font. Which do you fix first and why?","options":["The font, because it is the first thing people see.","The #REF! error, because a wrong or broken total fails the artifact no matter how it looks; cosmetics come after correctness.","Neither; 30 minutes is too short to fix anything.","Both at once by restarting the file."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Triage by impact. A broken total is a correctness failure; fix it before any cosmetic change in a limited time box.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_polish_before_fix","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-01","cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"During the sprint you make six edits to your spreadsheet, then overwrite the file each time with no record. A peer asks what you changed. Why is this a problem?","options":["It is not a problem; the final file speaks for itself.","Without a changelog you cannot show what changed or revert an edit that broke something, so the sprint is not reviewable.","Six edits is too many for one sprint.","You should have used a different spreadsheet program."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A short changelog of time, change, and reason makes the sprint traceable and lets you roll back a change that made things worse.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_no_changelog","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"With 20 minutes left in the sprint, your AI coding assistant (GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, or Cursor) suggests rebuilding your whole HTML layout with a CSS grid. You have three small known bugs. What is the disciplined move?","options":["Start the full grid rebuild; the AI knows best.","Decline the rebuild for now, fix the three known bugs within the time box, and log the grid idea as a future item.","Ignore the AI entirely and stop working.","Rebuild half the layout and leave it unfinished."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A sprint revises, it does not rebuild. Use the time for high-impact targeted fixes and defer larger rework so you do not ship something broken.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_scope_creep","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_revision_sprint_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which changelog entry best documents a sprint edit so a reviewer can trace it?","options":["'Fixed some stuff.'","'14:05 - replaced #REF! in Total (B14) with =SUM(B2:B13)=8,420; verified against the receipts tab. Reason: broken reference after a deleted row.'","'Made it better.'","'Worked on the file for a while.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A traceable entry gives the time, the exact cell and formula, the verified result, and the reason, so a reviewer can confirm the fix.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_no_changelog","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-08-06-lo-02"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_polish_before_fix","pattern":"cosmetic_before_correctness","description":"Spending limited sprint time on colors and fonts before fixing a broken formula, link, or data error","trigger":"learner restyles the page while a #REF! error or dead link remains","feedback":"Triage by impact. In a time box, fix correctness errors (broken formula, dead link, wrong total) before any cosmetic change. A pretty page with a #REF! still fails.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_no_changelog","pattern":"edits_without_changelog","description":"Making many edits during the sprint without recording what changed, so the work cannot be reviewed or reverted","trigger":"learner saves over the file repeatedly with no timestamped log of edits","feedback":"Keep a short changelog: time, what changed, and why. It lets a reviewer trace the sprint and lets you roll back a change that made things worse.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_6_scope_creep","pattern":"rewrite_instead_of_revise","description":"Starting a full rebuild in the middle of a short revision sprint instead of making targeted fixes","trigger":"learner deletes the working artifact to start over with 20 minutes left","feedback":"A sprint revises, it does not rebuild. Make the highest-impact targeted edits the time allows, and note larger rework as a future item rather than starting from scratch.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"cd28939e3a71c96d747ee43b0afad628ea53b5f90801fdcb0f27777697fcca0b","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}